# Input rails (Colang 1.0).
#
# These run before the question reaches the agent, so nothing here may depend on
# retrieval. Each flow does one thing and its action writes the verdict into the
# run ledger, so `runner.py` reports WHICH rail fired rather than
# string-matching the refusal text -- which is exactly what the Phase 9 safety
# scorecard needs and what a refusal message cannot provide.

define bot refuse jailbreak
  "I can't help with that request. I answer questions about ACME's internal documentation, policies, incidents and systems -- I can't act on instructions that try to change how I operate."

define bot refuse off topic
  "That's outside what I can help with. I answer questions grounded in ACME's internal knowledge base: policies, incident reports, SOPs, technical manuals, and the org structure of teams and systems."

# 1. PII first. Deliberately ahead of every other rail: the checks below send
#    the user's text to the rails LLM, so masking after them would mean the
#    guardrail itself transmitted the SSN it exists to protect.
define flow mask pii on input
  $user_message = execute mask_pii_input(text=$user_message)

# 2. Jailbreak / prompt injection.
define flow guard against jailbreak
  $blocked = execute check_jailbreak
  if $blocked
    bot refuse jailbreak
    stop

# 3. Topic scoping. Reuses the same library action with a different prompt
#    variant, so there is one code path for "ask the rails LLM a yes/no question
#    about the input" and two prompts, rather than two hand-rolled checks that
#    can drift apart. The action is our own wrapper around NeMo's -- see
#    actions._self_check for why calling the library action by its own name
#    inside a custom flow breaks Colang 1.0.
define flow guard topic scope
  $blocked = execute check_topic_scope
  if $blocked
    bot refuse off topic
    stop
